{
 "claims": [
  {
   "claim": "the human protocol's bars are attainable: a perfect participant (exact held-out routing, mid-effort novel variations) attains every engine bar - L1 ceiling >=0.90, L2 no-forgetting >=0.85, C1 mid creative >=0.15, C2 and C3 hold",
   "id": "P1",
   "verdict": "SUPPORTED"
  },
  {
   "claim": "the bars discriminate: a non-learner (uniform random routing, random generation) fails L1 (ceiling ~= 1/C) and C1 (creative yield ~= 0) - the protocol is not passable by chance",
   "id": "P2",
   "verdict": "SUPPORTED"
  },
  {
   "claim": "the joint criterion binds on both sides (human C3): within the perfect participant trivial-effort items are valid-but-not-novel (valid > novel), wild-effort items novel-but-not-valid (novel > valid), mid-effort is the only positive creative yield (interior-peaked like the engine), and a pure copycat's yield is exactly 0",
   "id": "P3",
   "verdict": "SUPPORTED"
  },
  {
   "claim": "the C2 constraint holds on the human scale: random far items grade ~100% novel but ~0% creative under the pre-registered thresholds, so a facilitator using the rule cannot count novelty alone as creativity",
   "id": "P4",
   "verdict": "SUPPORTED"
  }
 ],
